; W7-3 (2026-08-30): emission_energy_multiplier 2.5 -> 0.55, against Talon's playtest notes 7
; AND 16. It is one number answering both, which is why they are one change.
;
; Note 7, verbatim: "The golden cubes, in the day, are overblown like too bright." At 2.5 the
; faces clip to a single flat yellow and the bloom halo runs ~40% past the silhouette, so the
; cube stops reading as a cube (docs/qa/W7-3/before/crate-day).
;
; Note 16, verbatim: the cube "will grow in size ... and if the player puts it back down again,
; the cube stays grown larger." MEASURED (docs/qa/W7-3/before/scale, one camera, three marks):
; nothing writes a durable scale. Carryable.PunchScale's 1.25x pickup pop springs back to 1
; within a second. What persists is this material's HIGHLIGHT: Carryable's pulse drives emission
; to base + pulse*(1 + base), so at base 2.5 a highlighted cube sits at 3.03-4.43 and its bloom
; swallows the geometry -- the t=16 frame shows the cube as a featureless BALL, edges gone, with
; the "E" prompt beside it. A scaled cube would still be a cube; a bloomed one is round. After a
; drop the player is by definition standing next to it, so it is highlighted exactly when they
; look at it, and it never goes back while they stand there.
;
; 0.55 keeps a legible highlight (0.78-1.40, i.e. 1.4-2.5x rest) with headroom to spare, and the
; highlighted value is now below what the cube used to sit at while IDLE. Emission alone casts
; no light in Forward+, so this satisfies THRILL-BIBLE Part II's 2026-08-30 ruling structurally:
; the cube may GLOW, it may not LIGHT -- a free, permanent, never-expiring lamp would defuse the
; camp glow stick and take Canopy-as-spatial-darkness and Landform-as-disorientation with it.
; ONE number, deliberately: a day-aware emission would be a second lighting authority for a crate.
[sub_resource type="StandardMaterial3D" id="StandardMaterial3D_crate"]
resource_local_to_scene = true
albedo_color = Color(0.87, 0.69, 0.13, 1)
roughness = 0.35
metallic = 0.4
emission_enabled = true
emission = Color(0.87, 0.69, 0.13, 1)
emission_energy_multiplier = 0.55

[node name="Crate" type="RigidBody3D"]
script = ExtResource("1_carryable")
kind = 0
tint = Color(0.87, 0.69, 0.13, 1)
; PHYS-1 (2026-09-20), ruling P4. The heaviest carryable and the one a player knocks things over
; WITH, so what matters here is that it keeps its momentum rather than spending it on the floor:
; friction 0.60 (on the shared PhysicsMaterial), and damping between the can's roll and the box's
; dead stop. A cube is not top-heavy and gets no centre-of-mass override.
physics_material_override = ExtResource("3_physmat")
linear_damp = 0.3
angular_damp = 1.0

[node name="Visual" type="Node3D" parent="."]

[node name="MeshInstance3D" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="Visual"]
mesh = SubResource("BoxMesh_crate")
material_override = SubResource("StandardMaterial3D_crate")

[node name="CollisionShape3D" type="CollisionShape3D" parent="."]
shape = SubResource("BoxShape3D_crate")

; CARRY-1 (2026-09-19) — THE FEEL COMPONENT. Every prop in this game is a RigidBody3D carrying
; BOTH NetworkedProp (its authority: who holds it, where the server says it is) and Interactable
; (its feel: how heavy it reads in the hand, where it rides, how it is posed). The split is the
; whole architecture — the authority layer never asks how a thing feels and the feel layer never
; decides who owns it — and it only works if both are actually on the prop.
;
; What it is read for here: HeftKg (this body's own Mass, times HeftScale) is the single number
; the carry spring turns into lag, droop, swing and squash, so a heavy prop lags in the hand AND
; thuds when it lands from the same value. HoldOffset/HoldRotationDegrees are where and how it
; rides. Nothing else on this component runs in-game: the hover hull is the lab's, and a
; networked grab goes through Carryable, never through Interactable.OnGrabbed.

[node name="Interactable" type="Node" parent="."]
script = ExtResource("2_interactable")
